<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aspar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 400–471</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Flavius Ardabur Aspar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Flavius Ardabur Aspar was an Eastern Roman general of Alan descent who dominated the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> for much of the mid-fifth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consul</a> in 434 and long a senior commander, he helped place the emperors Marcian in 450 and Leo I in 457 on the throne, but as an Arian Christian of barbarian origin could not rule himself. Leo I had him and his son Ardabur murdered in 471.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aspar.jpg" alt="Aspar" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: sailko · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piatto_di_ardaburio,_argento_fuso,_434_d.c._(found_in_1769)_03.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alan soldiers and commanders rose high in Roman service; the general <b>Aspar</b>, of Alan descent, was one of the most powerful men in the Eastern Roman Empire in the mid-fifth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alans">Alans · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Dengizich attacked the Romans in 467 without his brother Ernak, was besieged, and was defeated by the general <b>Aspar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Marcian, a domesticus who had served the commanders Ardabur and <b>Aspar</b> for fifteen years, was elected and inaugurated emperor of the East after the death of Theodosius II.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Backed by the powerful general <b>Aspar</b>, Leo I became emperor in Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-7">7 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
